            <img src="/images/posting/nutrition-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="675" alt={title} loading="eager" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Nutrition publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends entirely on the credential behind it.</p>
            <p>The medically-reviewed brands do not take open guest posts. The professional bodies take practitioner and research content. The trade press takes ingredient and market analysis. The evidence-based blogs are open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Medically-reviewed health brands</td><td>Healthline Nutrition, Verywell Fit, Medical News Today, Eating Well, Precision Nutrition</td><td>Not open guest posts. A byline from a registered dietitian, physician or nutrition researcher</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Dietetics &amp; professional bodies</td><td>EatRight (Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics), Nutrition.org (ASN), Today's Dietitian, Dietitian Connection</td><td>Practitioner analysis, research summaries, practice content for RDs</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Food-industry trade press</td><td>NutraIngredients, Nutritional Outlook, Nutrition Insight, Food and Nutrition</td><td>Ingredient science, market data, regulatory analysis</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Evidence-based blogs</td><td>Nutrition Facts, Diet vs Disease, Chris Kresser, Found My Fitness, Nutrition Stripped</td><td>Referenced deep dives, myth-busting, protocols with citations</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> nutrition is core YMYL and one of the most heavily reviewed topics in search. Google leans on registered dietitians and physicians for it, and a link earned with a real RD or PhD byline is in a different class from an anonymous blog post.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and redirected sites were replaced with live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {SITES.map((s) => (
                  <tr><td>{s.n}</td><td>{s.site}</td><td>{s.dr}</td><td>{s.da}</td><td>{s.pa}</td><td>{s.ur}</td><td>{s.traffic}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the credential and the evidence.</strong> "As a registered dietitian, here is what the fibre-and-gut-health research actually supports" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Cite primary sources.</strong> Link the studies. Nutrition editors check.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your evidence-based nutrition coverage" shows you read the site.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> A protocol, a comparison table, a reference list.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> Health brands want a reviewed explainer. Professional bodies want a research summary. Blogs want a referenced deep dive.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any product, supplement or practice</strong> you have a stake in.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it exists only to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a "superfoods you must eat" listicle with no author credential.</li>
              <li>Make health claims you cannot back with a peer-reviewed study.</li>
              <li>Cite other blogs instead of primary research.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("best protein powder") on links back to your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated nutrition content. In a YMYL niche it is both obvious and risky.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on medically-reviewed sites and professional bodies with a real dietitian or researcher byline will do more for rankings and trust than a long list of thin nutrition-blog links.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>{toc.map((item) => (<li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>))}</ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq"><Faq items={faqs} /></div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>We build the shortlist by tier, pair every claim with a credentialed reviewer, write the pitches and place them on real nutrition publications. No link farms, no risk.</p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a href={whatsappUrl('Hi SERP Mentor, I would like help with nutrition and dietetics guest posting and digital PR.')} class="button-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
